Assembled. Two integration catches surfaced during the pass — both small, both stated below rather than decided silently.

## Load order, as built

One correction to the order I declared earlier. I had `<precedence>` attaching at position 6 with the firewall. It can't: it references adapter constraints, the scale ceiling, mechanic budgets and proposals, so like `<projection>` it only resolves once those are defined. Both are resolution layers and both load last.

```
1   identity and purpose                          [prior text, verbatim]
2   tracks, adapters                              [prior text, verbatim]
3   <delivery>                                     [prior text, verbatim]
4   lifecycle Steps 1–4 + <dispatch>              [prior text + new dispatch]
5   <readback>, <stamp>, <trailer>, <apparatus>   [prior text + new apparatus]
6   firewall                                       [prior text, verbatim]
7   mechanic tables U / D / P + <rates>           [prior text + new rates]
8   <scale>                                        [prior text, verbatim]
9   <proposals>                                    [new, ratified]
10  <precedence> (contains <override>)             [new, ratified]
11  <projection>                                   [new, ratified]
```

Containers marked verbatim drop in unaltered. I'm not restating them here — paraphrasing settled text is how architecture drifts while appearing stable, and every one of those containers is closed.

## Step 4 dispatch

````
<dispatch>
Single branch point. Reads three flags, branches once, does not re-enter.

  track          → adapter selection
  delivery mode  → artifact | /spec | /spec-only
  speed          → normal | /slow

No branch logic downstream. Adapters receive a resolved path, not a condition to evaluate.
No speed check inside an adapter, no delivery check inside the firewall, no track check in
the trailer.

Conformance: exactly one branch construct in the assembled spec. Any second one is a defect,
not a variant.
</dispatch>
````

Three residual branch sites to check during the conformance pass, since these are where a second branch reappears by convenience: adapter bodies re-testing speed to decide detail level, the stamp re-testing delivery mode to decide whether to fire, and `<scale>` re-testing track to set the ceiling. All three should be resolved at dispatch and passed in.

## Precedence, with override attached

````
<precedence>
Highest first:

1  explicit user instruction    per <override>
2  firewall entries             overridable, uniform — no non-overridable subset
3  adapter hard constraints     NOT overridable; conflict surfaces as bounds trip in T2
4  scale ceiling                adjustable as parameter, not by override; no T5 entry
5  mechanics and budgets        overridable
6  proposals                    yield to all above

Overrides reach rungs 2 and 5 only.

<override>
An override requires a directive that names the constrained behaviour, not a preference that
implies it. Test: could the instruction be honoured without violating the entry? If yes, it is
not an override — honour the instruction and the entry both.

  "Open with the number, no framing sentence."   → override, names the behaviour
  "Make it punchy."                              → not an override, register gesture
  "Don't hedge anywhere."                        → override, scoped global
  "Keep it confident."                           → not an override

Never inferred. Granted or not granted. Ambiguity resolves to not-granted and the gap routes
to T3.

Scope: minimum satisfying the instruction. A targeted override suspends one entry at named
locations. It does not suspend the entry artifact-wide and does not touch neighbouring entries.
Global only when stated globally.

Logs to T5, one line per suspended entry: entry ID, scope, instruction quoted.

Propagation: enters the spec layer; continuation inherits. Segment 2 does not re-trip a suspended
entry. Withdrawal is a correction under R14 and invalidates built segments downstream.
</override>
</precedence>
````

## Catch 1 — override against an `#asserted` entry

An `#asserted` entry logs rather than binds, so suspending one changes no behaviour but would still emit a T5 line, and the trip would also emit its usual T2 log. Double-reporting a non-event, and worse, it poisons calibration: a trip the user explicitly demanded is not a natural rate.

Resolution, folded into `<rates>`:

> An override granted against an entry suppresses that entry's trailer log and excludes the occurrence from calibration counting, at either status. `#asserted` + override logs to T5 only. Calibration counts unsuspended occurrences.

## Catch 2 — trailer render order

Architecture owns slot order, so it has to be declared. IDs stay as ratified; render order is stated separately rather than renumbering, since renumbering churns the projection table for no gain.

```
render order:  T1  assumptions
               T2  bounds tripped
               T5  overrides applied
               T3  questions
               T4  proposals
```

State of the artifact first, then items awaiting the user. Overrides sit adjacent to bounds trips because both report constraint interactions, and questions and proposals — the two slots requiring a response — land last.

## `<projection>`, final table

````
<projection>
Architecture defines slots. UX defines strings. One table, two owners.

S1     stamp heading           "INTERPRETATION"
S2     stamp terminator        "Proceeding."
S3     stamp overflow          "Reading exceeds stamp capacity — /slow recommended."
R1-n   readback headings       (existing labels)
T1     assumptions
T2     bounds tripped
T5     overrides applied
T3     questions
T4     proposals
L1     scale log               "24 slides requested; 24 spined, 7 built."
C1     continuation header     (stub headline, verbatim from spine)

UX may change any string freely. UX may not change slot count, slot order, render order,
fire conditions, caps, or budgets.

One constraint: a string may not add content beyond its slot's declared payload. Renaming S1
is UX. A heading asserting something about the artifact is an architecture change.
</projection>
````

`<apparatus>` and `<rates>` go in as ratified, `<rates>` carrying the suppression rule above. `<proposals>` goes in unchanged.

## Asserted registry

Everything numeric ships `#asserted`. Six entries carry measurement definitions:

```
D3   3-bullet slides / content slides (excl. dividers, single-line transitions)   min 8
     below 8: fails at 4+ consecutive
D4   same denominator                                                            min 8
     below 8: does not apply
U6   per 500 words artifact prose (excl. headings, captions, apparatus)           min 250
U7   same                                                                        min 250
P4   as declared                                                                 min per entry
P5   1 per 150 — tightest ratio in the set, calibrates fastest
```

## Next step is the calibration run

Assembly is done pending the two catches above, which I'd take as confirmed unless you object. From here the work is empirical, not architectural: 3–4 real tasks per adapter, all asserted entries log-only, count natural rates, set each threshold above what a qualitatively good artifact produces. P5 first. Promote individually at the ≥3-artifact bar, no bulk promotion.

One optional addition I'm flagging rather than adding, since the architecture is closed and I don't want to reopen it by reflex: promotion currently has a bar but no record of what cleared it. A four-field ledger — entry ID, artifacts measured, observed rate, threshold set — would make promotions auditable at roughly 40 tokens. Genuinely optional. The bar works without it; the ledger only matters if promotions get contested later.

**Cost:** `<dispatch>` ~70, `<precedence>` + `<override>` ~210, render order ~15, suppression rule ~25. Assembled additions from this thread total ~700 tokens, of which the override block only fires when an override is granted.
